**Issue L — Consistency: the update_branch site checks `machinePushVf` BEFORE `freshPushVf`, backstop requires `vprFreshPush && !vprMachinePush`.** Equivalent logic (both require fresh && !machine). Fine.

**Issue M — one more subtle thing in the update_branch site:** the fail-closed branch condition changed from `lastCommitter === null || actorVf === null || parkedAtVf === null` to `actorVf === null || parkedAtVf === null`. Since lastCommitter is now derived from actorVf, equivalent. OK.

**Issue N — walk-per-tick cost:** each walk step = 1 gh api call. Two probe sites per parked PR per relevant rule. update_branch processes up to 20 (limit) BEHIND PRs; parked ones with bot merges walk 2 calls typically. Acceptable. SUGGESTION: could cap known by combining... not worth it.

**Issue O — Test-shape drift:** Pre-existing tests (202, 204) feed `committers` stubs which are now dead — the probe no longer issues the plain `/commits/<sha>` command; those tests now pass via the fail-closed path rather than the machine-hold path. The `vfCli` docblock still documents "the committer-name probe" which no longer exists. This is a test-quality/maintainability nit (IMPORTANT or SUGGESTION?). The tests still assert the right OUTCOME (park holds). But they no longer verify the stated mechanism ("last committer sm-silent-update" hold vs "probe failed" hold). Given the repo culture of precise ownership notes and mechanism-level tests, I'd call this ⚠️ IMPORTANT — update those stubs to the actor shape so the tests exercise the intended machine-hold branch, not fail-closed. Hmm, let me verify claim that they now take the fail-closed path.

